<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carmarthenshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carmarthenshire is a county in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, the largest of the historic Welsh counties, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthen">Carmarthen</a> as its county town and Llanelli as its largest town. It covers the valley of the Tywi and the uplands around it. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the core of the kingdoms of Ystrad Tywi and Deheubarth, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> mined gold at Dolaucothi in its north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carmarthenshire.jpg" alt="Carmarthenshire" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Hurrell · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guildhall_Square,_Carmarthen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Carmarthenshire is bounded by Ceredigion to the north, Powys, Neath Port Talbot and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swansea">Swansea</a> to the east, the Bristol Channel to the south and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a> to the west. Much of it is upland, with the Black Mountain range in the east and the foothills of the Cambrian Mountains north of the Towy valley. The county top is Fan Foel at 781 metres. The River Towy, the longest river flowing entirely within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, drains it, and the best farmland lies in the broad Tywi Valley, which gives the county its name the Garden of Wales.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">Stone tools</a> from Coygan Cave near Laugharne show hominins, probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neanderthal">Neanderthals</a>, in the area at least 40,000 years ago. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> the land was part of the kingdom of the Demetae, one of whose chief settlements, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthen">Moridunum</a>, is now Carmarthen. The Roman fort at Carmarthen dates from around 75 CE, and with a Roman amphitheatre nearby this probably makes it the oldest continually occupied town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. In the High Middle Ages the area was part of the Kingdom of Deheubarth, with its court at Dinefwr, and then disputed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> and Welsh lords, with many castles changing hands several times.</p><p>After Edward I's conquest, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-rhuddlan">Statute of Rhuddlan</a> reorganised the region in 1284 into Carmarthenshire, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthen">Carmarthen</a> as its capital and the seat of his courts of chancery and exchequer. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> made Carmarthen an international trading port, the only staple port in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. Owain Glyndŵr captured Carmarthen Castle in 1405. The Parliamentarians recaptured it in 1648 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> ordered it slighted.</p><p>The first industrial canal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> was built in 1768 to carry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> from the Gwendraeth Valley to the coast, and by the end of the nineteenth century Llanelli was the world centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>-plating. Around 1840 Carmarthenshire was at the centre of the Rebecca Riots, when farmers and agricultural workers dressed as women rebelled against higher taxes and tolls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmarthen">Carmarthen</a> is the county town, though Llanelli is by far the largest town. The county has the largest area of the historic counties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> and its population was 183,777 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Carn Goch, near Llandeilo, is one of the most impressive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> forts, and the county has castles at Carreg Cennen, Dinefwr, Kidwelly, Laugharne and elsewhere. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolaucothi-gold-mines">Dolaucothi Gold Mines</a> were worked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and Laugharne is associated with the poet Dylan Thomas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carmarthenshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Demetae lived in what is now Pembrokeshire and western <b>Carmarthenshire</b>, a region of small defended farmsteads, known as raths, and coastal promontory forts.</blockquote><a href="/state/demetae">Demetae</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom covered much of later Cardiganshire and <b>Carmarthenshire</b>, a land of upland pasture and coastal farms with important churches such as Llanbadarn Fawr.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-seisyllwg">Kingdom of Seisyllwg</a></li><li><blockquote>Ystrad Tywi, 'the vale of the Tywi', covered the fertile river valley and surrounding uplands of what is now <b>Carmarthenshire</b>, including the old Roman town of Moridunum, Carmarthen.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ystrad-tywi">Kingdom of Ystrad Tywi</a></li></ul></section>
